.TH CTPOOL_DESTROY 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ctpool_destroy \- destroy a thread pool and invalidate the handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

ctpool_destroy(pool);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Drains or cancels every task that is still pending, then joins every
worker thread, waits for every resolved use of the handle that is still
in flight to stop, and frees every resource of the pool. The macro then
sets the pool handle to CTPOOL_INVALID.

When the caller called neither ctpool_shutdown_drain() nor
ctpool_shutdown_immediate() beforehand, the macro runs a drain shutdown
by itself, so every task in the queue finishes before the library frees
the pool.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
pool  Thread pool variable to destroy (set to CTPOOL_INVALID after
      destruction).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
It is safe to call when pool is CTPOOL_INVALID; the macro then does
nothing.

A call on a stale handle (one that an earlier call already destroyed,
or that another thread destroys at the same time) is a FATAL ERROR,
which is abort() and SIGABRT. This is never a silent double free: the
handle carries a generation tag that the library checks before it
touches the pool, so it always detects such a value and never
dereferences it.

A call on pool from inside a task that runs on one of the worker threads
of pool, or from the on_complete callback of that task, is a FATAL ERROR
as well, for a related reason: that worker is still on its way back
through its own dispatch loop, and would touch the mutex, the condition
variables and the struct of the pool again immediately after this call
freed them. Leave the destroy to another thread instead, or run it after
the task returns.

The macro evaluates pool exactly once. It must be a modifiable lvalue,
such as a variable or an element of an array, so an expression with a
side effect, such as handles[i++], destroys and clears one element.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_construct (3),
.BR ctpool_shutdown_drain (3),
.BR ctpool_shutdown_immediate (3)
